        id: 1,
        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "An industrial fabricator entered into a written contract with an agricultural machinery company to manufacture and deliver 50 heavy-duty hydraulic lifting cylinders for $100,000, payable upon delivery scheduled for October 1. The contract explicitly provided that all units were to be manufactured strictly using American-made seamless DOM steel tubing. On September 20, the fabricator completed assembly and tendered delivery of all 50 cylinders at the buyer's loading dock. The buyer inspected the shipment and discovered that while all 50 cylinders conformed in performance, dimensions, and pressure tolerance, 10 of the cylinders were fabricated using imported German seamless tubing of identical metallurgical grade. The buyer properly rejected the entire shipment under the perfect tender rule. On September 21, the fabricator gave seasonable written notice of its intention to cure by replacing the 10 cylinders with American-steel units by October 1. Before the fabricator could tender the replacement cylinders on October 1, the agricultural machinery company's main assembly plant was completely destroyed by an accidental fire, leaving the buyer with no immediate use for the cylinders. The buyer notified the fabricator that the contract was terminated by impossibility and refused to permit the tender of cure.",
