        fp: "A manufacturer of high-voltage industrial electrical switches designed an outdoor disconnect switch housing intended for electric power substations. To protect maintenance workers, industry safety guidelines published by the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) required all such switch enclosures to feature an internal mechanical interlock that automatically grounds the high-voltage bus whenever the outer access door is opened. The manufacturer omitted the mechanical grounding interlock, instead placing a high-visibility, yellow reflective warning label on the exterior door: 'DANGER: HIGH VOLTAGE. TURN OFF AUXILIARY POWER AND MANUALLY INSERT GROUNDING ROD BEFORE OPENING.' A utility technician employed by an electric power company was dispatched to inspect the switch during an emergency blackout. Rushing to restore power to a hospital, the technician opened the enclosure door without inserting the manual grounding rod. An electrical arc of 13,800 volts flashed across the cabinet, severely burning the technician and causing permanent blindness. In a pure comparative fault jurisdiction, the technician brought a strict products liability design defect action against the switch manufacturer.",
